.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_QUERY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_query \- look up all values for a query parameter
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

const char **chttpsvr_req_query(chttpsvr_req *req, const char *key,
                                 size_t *count_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Parses the query string lazily, on the first call.  It then returns an
array of every URL-decoded value that belongs to key.  That array ends
with a NULL.  The library URL-decodes each key of the query string and
compares it with key as it stands, as url.Values of the Go net/url
package does, so pass the decoded form: "a b" finds both a+b and a%20b,
and "a%20b" finds neither.  It stores the count in count_out, when count_out is not
NULL.  It returns NULL when the key is absent.

The value strings that the array elements point to stay valid for the
length of the handler call.  The array pointer itself is a scratch
buffer for this one request.  Every call to chttpsvr_req_query reuses
that buffer.  Copy the pointers that you need out of the array before
you make another call to chttpsvr_req_query on the same request.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This function cannot tell an out-of-memory failure apart from a real
"key is absent" result.  The failure can happen while the library
parses the query string, and it can happen while the library grows the
result array.  Both conditions give NULL and a count of 0.  Call
chttpsvr_req_query_oom(req) after a NULL return to tell the two cases
apart.  Or use chttpsvr_req_query_one for a key that has one value.
That function returns ccol_not_enough_memory when it runs out of
memory.

A component of the query string that has a "=" but an empty key half,
for example "?=hello", is a real pair that you can look up: the key ""
maps to the value "hello".  A component that is completely empty is
different.  The parser skips it outright, and does not read it as a
false entry with an empty key and an empty value.  A component is
completely empty when there is nothing at all between two "&"
characters, before an "&" at the start, or after an "&" at the end.
"?a=1&&b=2" and "?&a=1" are two examples.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_query_one (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_query_oom (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_raw_query (3)
